WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.979 --> 00:00:06.140
Former Dunder Mifflin paper salesman and assistant
to the regional manager Dwight Schrute is

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:09.540
loud, intense, and one of television's most
memorable dunces.

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:14.020
However, even though the titular office's
doors have been shuttered for a while now,

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:19.130
there's still a lot you might not know about
the beet-loving, karate-kicking owner of Schrute

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:19.620
Farms.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.460
Dwight Schrute and Rainn Wilson seem as inseparable
as …

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:24.560
"Bears.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:25.740
Beets.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:27.480
Battlestar Galactica."

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:33.160
As difficult as it is to imagine anyone other
than Wilson playing Dunder Mifflin Scranton's

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:37.820
fire safety chief, it's utterly impossible
to contemplate what would've happened if one

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:41.360
famous comedian who auditioned for the role
had actually gotten it.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:46.540
In 2013, the final season of The Office was
released on DVD, and included in the extras

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:50.500
is a short video revealing some of the actors
who auditioned for the series.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.410
Among other noteworthy surprises, Bob Odenkirk
of Better Call Saul fame auditioned for the

00:00:55.410 --> 00:01:00.870
role of Michael Scott, Kathryn Hahn tried
out for Pam, and both John Cho and Adam Scott

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:02.160
took a crack at Jim Halpert.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:07.300
But perhaps most surprising of all is that
Seth Rogen tried his best to land the part

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.300
of Dwight Schrute.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:09.940
"Urine is sterile.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:11.360
Did you know that?

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:16.140
If you're in the field, you can clean a man's
wound by taking a wiz on it.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.500
That is actually in first aid books written
by the Red Cross."

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:24.550
What's most fascinating about the clip is
how apparent it is that Rogen's Dwight wouldn't

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:27.940
have been within a galaxy of the same character
Wilson gave us.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.240
Reading the same line, Dwight would be obnoxious
and condescending, speaking as if he were

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.080
delivering the lesson to a child.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:40.060
Rogen seems absolutely likable, not very bright,
and not at all Schrutish — kind of like

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:44.220
Kevin the accountant if he knew a few more
factoids and was awake for more of the day.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.500
Throughout the course of The Office, Dwight
Schrute both worships Michael Scott and has

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.000
ambitions for his office.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:55.600
And in one of the many ways in which life
imitates art, Rainn Wilson didn't initially

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.540
audition for the role of assistant to the
regional manager.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.360
He wanted to be the big dog himself.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:05.280
Much like Bob Odenkirk, Wilson went into his
audition taking aim at the role of everybody's

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.720
favorite regional manager, Michael Scott.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:13.450
Alas, it wasn't to be, possibly because, as
Wilson puts it, his audition amounted to a,

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:15.820
quote, "terrible [Ricky] Gervais impersonation."

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.430
"Yeah, I originally, uhh…

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:21.390
I auditioned for both the Michael Scott role
— the Steve Carrel role — and the Dwight

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:21.880
role.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:25.420
But my audition for the Michael Scott role
was just terrible."

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:31.140
Considering how amazing Wilson is as Schrute,
as well as Steve Carell's success as the equally

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.060
clueless Michael Scott, it's probably for
the best.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:38.480
It might be interesting to take a peek in
the alternate reality where Wilson got cast

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:39.480
as Michael Scott.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:45.640
In January 2012, Deadline reported NBC was
considering an Office spin-off with Rainn

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:47.200
Wilson starring as Dwight Schrute.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.239
The Farm would've been a family comedy in
a setting Office fans had grown familiar with

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:53.160
over the years: Schrute Farms.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:57.440
Among others, the series would have featured
Matt Jones — a.k.a. Badger from Breaking

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:03.920
Bad — as Dwight's cousin Zeke, veteran character
actor Tom Bower as Dwight's Nazi Uncle Heinrich,

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:08.920
Thomas Middleditch of Silicon Valley fame
as Dwight's Bigfoot-hunting brother Jeb, and

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:12.980
Venezuelan-born Majandra Delfino as Dwight's
sister Fannie.

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:17.280
Of course, Michael Schur would reprise his
role as Dwight's creepy, neck-bearded cousin

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:17.880
Mose.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:19.280
"Ahhh!"

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:19.880
"Ahhh!"

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:21.300
"You remember my cousin Mose?"

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:22.540
"Welcome children."

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.360
"Were you painting in the dark?"

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:27.379
For better or worse, The Farm wasn't meant
to be.

00:03:27.379 --> 00:03:32.040
In October 2012, Wilson tweeted the news that
NBC had passed on the series, although he'd,

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:33.880
quote, "had a blast making the pilot."

00:03:34.209 --> 00:03:38.319
The pilot for The Farm was then recut as a
single Office episode in the show's final

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:38.820
season.

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:41.800
But would that story have worked as a full-fledged
series?

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.240
It's hard to say, but Vulture writer Matt
Schimkowitz notes that Dwight's popularity

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:49.500
came mainly from him acting as an antagonist
to Jim Halpert.

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:53.880
Making him the lead in a comedy series about
a disjointed family that he's expected to

00:03:53.889 --> 00:03:59.859
bring together changes Dwight's role dramatically
to, quote, "the likable hero we've never seen."

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:02.740
In other words, maybe it's best that we never
visited The Farm.

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:08.720
From his obsession with having a "Second Second
Life" to his epic sales struggle with a supposedly

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:12.860
self-aware A.I., Dwight is well-known for
some of his online shenanigans.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:17.060
What you might not know is that Rainn Wilson
used to maintain a blog in Dwight's voice.

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:22.110
Unfortunately, it doesn't exist anymore, but
NBC used to host Schrute Space, which let

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:27.500
fans read Dwight's thoughts on different Scranton
radio stations or his invaluable martial arts

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:28.000
tips.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:32.880
While NBC eventually opened the blog up to
the masses, it started as something that existed

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:34.950
exclusively on the set of The Office.

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:39.170
The computers on The Office's set were networked
together, and Wilson would write the Dwight-voiced

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:42.720
blog while scenes were being filmed so the
rest of the cast could enjoy it.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:46.120
Wilson told The Morning Call that during the
filming of the pilot episode, quote, "The

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:50.160
producers saw [the blog] when they walked
by and thought it was funny."

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:54.080
A few emails later between NBC and Wilson,
and Schrute Space was born.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:58.020
Now, if only NBC would bring it back, the
world would be a better place.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:00.720
Oh well — there's always Creed's blog…

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:06.700
"Www.creedthoughts.gov.www\creedthoughts.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.140
Check it out."

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:15.460
In 2015, Rainn Wilson's memoir The Bassoon
King: Art, Idiocy, and Other Assorted Tales

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:19.630
from the Band Room was released, and like
many nonfiction books, a different author

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:21.320
was invited to write the foreword.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:26.070
Namely, the foreword is credited to "Dwight
Kurt Schrute," and it's written entirely in

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:27.720
Dwight's disdainful voice.

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:32.020
Schrute spends the first bit of the foreword
discussing why he's agreed to write it, which

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:33.280
he clearly doesn't want to do.

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:34.200
He explains:

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.300
"When someone asks me to do a task, the first
thing I do is determine whether the request

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:39.420
is some sort of trick."

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:45.260
While the job of writing the foreword survives
Dwight's vetting process, he tells us he doesn't

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:50.610
like The Bassoon King or any books that involve,
quote, "'funny stories' regarding some stupid

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:54.340
actor..." and then he promptly proceeds to
list a long number of exceptions to this which

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:59.870
includes books by Charles Bronson, any Game
of Thrones cast member, Sam Neill, and Dolph

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:00.620
Lundgren.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:05.120
Dwight calls Rainn Wilson a "laughable idiot,"
and eventually reveals he has only agreed

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:06.650
to write the foreword for the money.

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:10.930
Before turning things over to Wilson, Dwight
gives us some other book ideas like Mennonite

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:16.430
Ghost Stories, a "fun games for kids" book
titled Hold This Book over a Candle, and the

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:19.920
revealing Conspiracy Theories: Who's Really
Behind Them?

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.940
Seriously, we'll take a whole book written
by Dwight, please.

